From 84ed8d9ce02fc3309cbeef76c4c9f0389c2e944b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 07:29:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Bloom and a colour grade: the new look's own full-screen pass

Under the new look the world camera gets a full-screen pass of its own and Bevy's bloom. The pass
grades the frame in linear light from the zone grade's values for the hour (0051): a white
balance that warms the highlights and cools the shadows (each tint normalised so it shifts hue,
not brightness), contrast around mid-grey in log space, and saturation around luminance. Elwynn's
golden hour comes out warmer and richer, its night cooler and a touch greyer, and outside a
graded zone the values are neutral and the pass does not run at all. Bloom is Bevy's own on the
world camera, additive, with a prefilter at 0.8 (softness 0.3) so only the brightest parts glow
(the sun, spells, lit windows), at the grade's intensity; it is taken off the camera entirely
when the grade asks for none and under the classic look.

The pass runs before the 1.12 glow, not after it: the world camera's main texture still holds the
gamma bytes there (the frame's one decode is the FFXGlow combine, drawn as the UI camera's
ground), so the pass decodes, grades and encodes back, the glow blurs the graded frame, and every
combine path reads it. Bevy's bloom runs after the glow on the same frame, as it does under
`Tonemapping::None` with `Hdr`. The shader is validated and translated by naga in the app's shader
test, the grade has a CPU twin with its own tests, and `worldLook 0` leaves the camera exactly as
it was.
